B.a32=s(["Understand what a Python program is","Use the print() function"],t.s)
B.a0b=s(["Print the text: Hello, World!"],t.s)
B.a3l=s(["Text must be in quotes: print('Hello, World!')"],t.s)
B.YY=new A.cw("Think of Python as a super-fast translator. It takes the simple instructions you write and turns them into something a computer can follow instantly.",null)
B.Z1=new A.cw("Every single line matters. Python reads top-to-bottom, one at a time \u2014 just like you're reading this. It finishes line one before it even glances at line two.",null)
B.YL=new A.cw("To make Python say something out loud, use `print()` and put your text inside quote marks. The quotes are how Python knows the words are for it to say, not instructions to follow.",'print("Hello, World!")')
B.Za=new A.cw("That's it. One line, one job. In the next step you'll write it yourself \u2014 and it will be the first program you've ever run.",null)
B.a2J=s([B.YY,B.Z1,B.YL,B.Za],t.xu)
B.Zi=new A.kc(B.a2J)
B.TK=new A.dm("ex-hello-world","lesson-1","Hello, World!","Use print() to display a friendly greeting. This is the traditional first program in every language.","# Write your code below\n",B.a32,B.a0b,B.a3l,1,B.M,null,B.Zi)
B.a2E=s(["Store values in variables","Combine text with variables"],t.s)
B.a1k=s(["Create a variable named name","Print Hello followed by the name"],t.s)
B.a_E=s(['A variable uses the = sign: name = "Kofi"'],t.s)
B.YM=new A.cw("A variable is a labelled box. You put something inside, give the box a name, and later you just say the name instead of repeating what was inside.",null)
B.YI=new A.cw('You fill a box with `=`. Whatever sits to the right gets stored under the name on the left. Python reads it right-to-left: it figures out `"Kofi"` first, then stores it as `name`.','name = "Kofi"')
B.Z5=new A.cw("Now the name works anywhere you'd use the value. One change to the box, and every place you mention it changes too.","name = 'Ama'\nprint('Hello', name)")
B.YS=new A.cw("Good variable names describe what's inside. `name` beats `x` \u2014 including for you, a week from now.",null)
B.a1z=s([B.YM,B.YI,B.Z5,B.YS],t.xu)
B.Zd=new A.kc(B.a1z)
B.TM=new A.dm("ex-variables","lesson-2","Variables and Names","Create a variable called name with your name, then print a greeting using it.","name = \nprint('Hello', name)\n",B.a2E,B.a1k,B.a_E,1,B.M,null,B.Zd)
B.a3v=s(["Use +, -, * and /","Understand operator precedence"],t.s)
B.a3m=s(["Print the sum of 7 and 5","Print the product of 7 and 5"],t.s)
B.a13=s(["print(a + b)","Multiplication uses the * symbol"],t.s)
B.YJ=new A.cw("Python does sums the way you'd write them. `+` adds, `-` takes away, `*` multiplies and `/` divides.","print(7 + 5)\nprint(7 - 5)\nprint(7 * 5)\nprint(7 / 5)")
B.YT=new A.cw("Watch the division though. `/` always gives a decimal, even when the answer is a whole number \u2014 `7 / 5` is `1.4`, not `1`.",null)
B.YU=new A.cw("Order matters, and Python follows the rules you learned in maths: multiply before you add. So `2 + 3 * 4` is `14`, not `20`. Use brackets when you want to change that.","print(2 + 3 * 4)\nprint((2 + 3) * 4)")
B.Z3=new A.cw("Variables work here too. Store your numbers first, then do the sum \u2014 that way changing a number later means changing it once.",null)
B.a03=s([B.YJ,B.YT,B.YU,B.Z3],t.xu)
B.Zj=new A.kc(B.a03)
B.TQ=new A.dm("ex-arithmetic","lesson-2","Arithmetic","Calculate and print the sum and product of two numbers.","a = 7\nb = 5\n",B.a3v,B.a3m,B.a13,1,B.M,null,B.Zj)
B.a18=s(["Use if / else","Compare values"],t.s)
B.a3C=s(["Use an if statement","Handle both outcomes"],t.s)
B.a0g=s(["if score >= 50:","Remember the colon at the end of the condition"],t.s)
B.YQ=new A.cw("So far your programs run the same lines every time. `if` lets them choose \u2014 like a fork in the road.",null)
B.Z2=new A.cw('An `if` asks a yes-or-no question. If the answer is yes, Python runs the indented lines underneath. If not, it skips them. The colon at the end says "the indented part belongs to me".','score = 72\nif score >= 50:\n    print("Pass")')
B.Z8=new A.cw('`>=` means "greater than or equal to". That little `=` is easy to forget, and forgetting it means a score of exactly 50 fails.',null)
B.YX=new A.cw("But what about the other answer? `else` is the second fork: Python takes the `if` branch when the question is true, and the `else` branch when it isn't.",'if score >= 50:\n    print("Pass")\nelse:\n    print("Try again")')
B.a0Q=s([B.YQ,B.Z2,B.Z8,B.YX],t.xu)
B.Zf=new A.kc(B.a0Q)
B.TP=new A.dm("ex-conditions","lesson-3","Making Decisions",'Given a score, print "Pass" when it is 50 or more, otherwise print "Try again".',"score = 72\n",B.a18,B.a3C,B.a0g,1,B.M,null,B.Zf)
B.a3o=s(["Use a for loop","Use the range() function"],t.s)
B.a1x=s(["Use a for loop","Print 1 through 5"],t.s)
B.a0t=s(["for i in range(1, 6):","range(1, 6) gives 1,2,3,4,5"],t.s)
B.Z6=new A.cw('Copying the same line five times works, until you need it a hundred times. A loop is how you say "do this again" without writing it again.',null)
B.Z9=new A.cw("`for` takes each item in a list, one at a time, and runs the indented lines for that item. `range(1, 6)` makes the list `1, 2, 3, 4, 5` \u2014 it stops *before* the last number, which catches everyone.","for i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i)")
B.YR=new A.cw("The `i` is just a name you choose. It starts as `1`, the loop runs, then it becomes `2`, runs again, and so on. On the last round it's `5`, and then the loop is done.",null)
B.YK=new A.cw("The indented lines are the loop's body \u2014 they run once per number. Put them at the wrong indent and Python will tell you, loudly.",null)
B.ZK=s([B.Z6,B.Z9,B.YR,B.YK],t.xu)
B.Zg=new A.kc(B.ZK)
B.TL=new A.dm("ex-loops","lesson-3","Loops","Print the numbers 1 to 5, one per line, using a loop.","# Use a for loop\n",B.a3o,B.a1x,B.a0t,1,B.M,null,B.Zg)
B.a19=s(["Define functions with def","Return values"],t.s)
B.a2T=s(["Define square(n)","Return n * n"],t.s)
B.a2u=s(["return n * n"],t.s)
B.YV=new A.cw("A function is a recipe you name. You write it once, then call it by name as often as you like \u2014 no rewriting the steps each time.",null)
B.YP=new A.cw("Start with `def`, then the name, then brackets. `n` is called a parameter: it's the ingredient the function needs. And the colon-and-indent rule applies exactly as it does with `if`.","def square(n):\n    return n * n")
B.Z_=new A.cw("Defining a function doesn't run it. Python just reads the recipe and remembers it. It only runs when you *call* it \u2014 brackets included.","def square(n):\n    return n * n\n\nprint(square(4))")
B.YW=new A.cw("`return` is what sends the answer back out. Everything after it belongs to the function and stops there. Without `return`, a function gives back nothing at all.",null)
B.a2N=s([B.YV,B.YP,B.Z_,B.YW],t.xu)
B.Ze=new A.kc(B.a2N)
B.TO=new A.dm("ex-functions","lesson-4","Reusable Functions","Write a function called square that returns a number multiplied by itself.","def square(n):\n    # your code here\n    pass\n",B.a19,B.a2T,B.a2u,1,B.M,null,B.Ze)
B.a2X=s(["Create lists","Iterate over lists"],t.s)
B.a_V=s(["Create a list literal","Print each fruit"],t.s)
B.a1Z=s(["fruits = ['mango', 'banana', 'orange']","for fruit in fruits:"],t.s)
B.Zb=new A.cw("A list is one box with many compartments. Instead of three separate names to remember, you keep them together in order.","fruits = ['mango', 'banana', 'orange']")
B.YN=new A.cw('Square brackets mean "this is a list", and commas keep the items apart. Python counts from zero, so the first fruit is at position `0`, not `1`.',"print(fruits[0])\nprint(fruits[2])")
B.YZ=new A.cw("A loop and a list are made for each other. `for fruit in fruits` hands you one item per round, already named \u2014 no counting positions at all.","for fruit in fruits:\n    print(fruit)")
B.Zc=new A.cw("Lists can change after you make one \u2014 add to it, remove from it, swap an item. That's what makes them more useful than plain variables.",null)
B.a35=s([B.Zb,B.YN,B.YZ,B.Zc],t.xu)
B.Zh=new A.kc(B.a35)
B.TR=new A.dm("ex-lists","lesson-4","Lists","Create a list of three fruits and print each one.","fruits = []\n",B.a2X,B.a_V,B.a1Z,1,B.M,null,B.Zh)
